  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure currently over Wisconsin will govern local conditions \nover the next several days, ensuring a dry and seasonable period for \nSE Michigan. Northerly flow maintains H8 temperatures in the low \nteens atop lake temperatures of 20+ C, which will be enough to keep \nlake moisture flux active over Lake Huron and generate pockets of \ndiurnal cumulus this afternoon. Outside of this, mostly sunny skies \nare expected. Lingering cyclonic influence from the New England low \nkeeps an elevated wind field aloft that the boundary layer will tap \ninto this afternoon. Breezy conditions with gusts up to 25 mph are \npossible. A very similar day is in store for Thursday as the surface \nhigh makes little eastward progress, although less breezy as the \nmore relaxed high pressure gradient gains influence. \n\nNext wave to impact the Great Lakes will be en route today and \nThursday as it rounds the crest of the 595 dam ridge over central \nCONUS. This will accelerate eastern progress of the ridge while also \nattempting to dislodge the thermal trough. The warm tongue will \ngradually lose strength though as it encounters deep troughing over \neastern CONUS, meaning a less aggressive warm up than what we saw \nearlier this month--into the upper 80s for Friday. \n\nThe wave then brings the next opportunity for showers and thunder to \nSE Michigan Friday night. Model consistency then breaks down for the \nweekend, differing in if the wave stays progressive or cuts off over \nthe Ohio Valley. Either way, SE Michigan looks to spend time under \nthe deformation axis to keep cloud cover and rain chances in the \nforecast for at least part of the weekend. Only a handful of dry \nmembers exist for Saturday in the 00z ensemble suite, although that \nnumber increases Sunday. Ensemble members also largely favor \ntemperatures to hold aob normal (mid-80s) for the weekend before a \nwarm up is expected early next week.",
